-### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Coordinate System Mismatch**: OpenCV and OpenGL conventions differ in Y and Z axes. Camera poses may need flipping: `c2w[:3, 1:3] *= -1` [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Out-of-Bounds Sampling**: UV coordinates outside $[0, W-1]$ or $[0, H-1]$ can cause NaNs. Use `padding_mode='border'` for depth and `padding_mode='zeros'` for masks [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Precision Errors**: Use a small epsilon ($\epsilon = 0.005$) when comparing depth to avoid culling points exactly on the surface due to floating-point inaccuracies [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Degenerate Input**: If a camera has no depth map or mask, skip that view [S1, S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### True Back-Face Culling via Group Masking
-A back-face culling technique that splits chunk meshes into 6 vertex pool buckets based on face orientation (groups 0-5) [S3, unofficial]. Draw calls are masked based on the camera position relative to the chunk, preventing the draw call from being issued entirely rather than culling in the fragment shader [S3, unofficial]. It is chosen when driver overhead must be minimized compared to splitting chunks into 6 sub-meshes [S3, unofficial]. Implementation involves inserting group IDs into a set based on camera position signs (e.g., if `cam::pos.x < 0` insert group 0) and masking the vertex pool [S3, unofficial].
+A back-face culling technique that splits chunk meshes into 6 vertex pool buckets based on face orientation (groups 0-5) [S3, unofficial]. Draw calls are masked based on the camera position relative to the chunk, preventing the draw call from being issued entirely rather than culling in the fragment shader [S3, unofficial]. This avoids the 6-fold driver overhead increase associated with splitting chunks into 6 sub-meshes [S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity**: $O(N \cdot V \cdot B)$, where $N$ is the number of points, $V$ is the number of validation frames, and $B$ is the batch size [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity**: $O(N \cdot V)$ for intermediate masks and valid counts [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths**: Effective at removing geometry occluded from all viewpoints; robust to complex scene geometry where local neighbor checks fail; leverages GPU parallelism for projection and sampling.
-*   **Limitations**: Requires pre-computed depth maps or masks; sensitive to camera pose accuracy; may remove valid geometry if the reference data is noisy or incomplete; higher memory usage for large meshes and many views.
-*   **When to Use**: When a set of high-quality reference views (depth/masks) is available; when global visibility is more important than local surface continuity.
-*   **When Not to Use**: When reference data is unavailable; when real-time performance is critical and pre-processing is not feasible; when the scene has large transparent or reflective surfaces that confuse depth sampling.
+- **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot V \cdot B)$, where $N$ is the number of points/vertices, $V$ is the number of validation frames (cameras), and $B$ is the batch size [S2, unofficial].
+- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N \cdot V)$ for intermediate masks and valid counts [S2, unofficial].
+- **Strengths:** Effective for complex, non-convex geometries where local neighbor checks fail; robust against self-occlusion; leverages GPU parallelism for projection and sampling.
+- **Limitations:** Requires pre-computed depth maps or masks for all views; sensitive to camera calibration errors; coordinate system mismatches (e.g., OpenCV vs OpenGL) can cause incorrect depth comparisons if not handled [S2, unofficial].
+- **When to use:** When generating static meshes from multi-view reconstructions (e.g., NeRF, Gaussian Splatting) where visibility is defined by the capture setup.
+- **When not to use:** For dynamic scenes with changing viewpoints where pre-computed masks are not feasible; for simple convex voxel structures where neighbor checks [Face Culling](8735-face-culling.md) are sufficient.
 
 ## Background
-Mask Projection Culling originates from techniques used in 3D reconstruction and Gaussian Splatting pipelines, where mesh quality is improved by filtering out noise and artifacts not consistent across multiple views [S1, S2, unofficial]. It is commonly used in differentiable rendering frameworks and NeRF-based reconstruction tools to extract clean meshes from implicit or point-based representations [S2, unofficial]. The method builds on the principle of multi-view stereo consistency, ensuring that only geometry visible from multiple angles is retained [S1, S2, unofficial].
+Mask Projection Culling originates from the post-processing steps of neural rendering pipelines, specifically 3D Gaussian Splatting and NeRF-based mesh extraction, where high-density point clouds or Gaussians need to be converted to manifold meshes [S1, S2, unofficial]. The technique was adapted to filter out "ghost" geometry and noise by ensuring that only geometry consistently visible across multiple training views is retained [S1, S2, unofficial]. The variant "True Back-Face Culling via Group Masking" is described in the context of high-performance voxel engines to optimize draw calls by leveraging vertex bucketing [S3, unofficial].
 
